  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Under accrual accounting, a credit sale is recorded as income when it is earned/invoiced, even though the cash is received later.",
      "sources": [
        "S3",
        "S4",
        "S5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Profit measures what a business earned over a period; cash flow tracks the actual movement of cash in and out. A statement of cash flows exists precisely because profit alone does not show an entity's ability to generate cash and meet obligations.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "Businesses need cash to pay suppliers and employees on time (operating cash outflows), and a profitable business can run into trouble if cash inflows and outflows are mistimed. This is one mechanism of failure, not the only one.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "type": "established",
      "text": "In ASIC's 2022-23 insolvency statistics, 'inadequate cash flow or high cash use' was the most frequently nominated cause of company failure, cited in 52% of registered liquidators' reports.",
      "sources": [
        "S6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "type": "illustrative",
      "text": "The company and all its figures are fictional and internally consistent: revenue 2.4M, expenses 1.9M, profit 0.5M, receivables 0.6M, cash 40k, bill due 120k.",
      "sources": []
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "type": "metaphor",
      "text": "Safe and vault (cash available now) versus a long road and pipes (money still owed or flowing out) are visual metaphors, not accounting definitions.",
      "sources": []
    }
  ],
